 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Architecture",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Two N-channel gate drivers, one ground-referred (LO) and one floating on HS (HO), each with its own logic input. Propagation delays of the two channels match within 8 ns max at +25 °C; dead time and drive protocol are left to the controller. A diode from VDD to HB is built in, so no external bootstrap diode is needed. The level shifter runs pulsed, for low power, and keeps the safe behaviour of a DC level shifter."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Inputs",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "HI and LI have CMOS thresholds (VIL 5.4 V, VIH 5.8 V typical at VDD = 12 V) with about 0.4 V of hysteresis and a 200 kΩ typical pulldown each. Because the inputs are independent, the part also serves topologies other than the half bridge. Any input pulse of 50 ns or longer reaches the output (tPW max)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Undervoltage protection",
   "page": 5,
   "text": "VDD and the bootstrap supply (VHB - VHS) each have an undervoltage lockout that holds the outputs low. VDD releases at 7.3 V typical rising with 0.5 V hysteresis; HB releases at 6.9 V typical rising with 0.4 V hysteresis. After a short dip of the high-side supply, HO comes back to the state set by HI."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Bootstrap supply",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "An external capacitor from HB to HS powers the high-side driver; the on-chip diode charges it from VDD. The diode's dynamic resistance is 0.8 Ω typical, its average current is limited to 100 mA, and the bootstrap supply may reach 114 VDC (HB absolute maximum +118 V)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Supply above 14 V",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "VDD is recommended at 9 V to 14 V. Above 14 V the part may run with derating: the allowed HS voltage falls from 100 V at VDD = 14 V to about 60 V at VDD = 16 V (Figure 16). The absolute maximum for VDD and VHB - VHS is 18 V."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Switch node",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "HO and LO are unaffected by supply glitches, by HS ringing below ground, and by HS slewing fast; the recommended HS slew rate is below 50 V/ns and HS may swing to -5 V on repetitive transients."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Output drive",
   "page": 6,
   "text": "Each output sources and sinks 2 A peak and has about 3 Ω output resistance. A 1000 pF load switches in 10 ns typical; a 0.1 µF load rises or falls from 3 V to 9 V in 0.5 µs typical."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Timing definitions",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "Propagation delays run from an input edge to the matching output edge (Figure 3). Delay matching (Figure 4) is taken with LI and HI driven in opposition: tMON is the time between HO turning off and LO turning on, tMOFF the time between LO turning off and HO turning on."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Exposed pad and packages",
   "page": 3,
   "text": "EPSOIC and QFN have an exposed pad isolated from all pins; it may go to ground or float, and the best thermal result comes from soldering it to the power ground plane. SOIC and EPSOIC meet the IPC-2221 conductor spacing guidelines for 100 V. The QFN follows JEDEC MO-220."
  },
